Thanks, Chris. Some follow-up clarification and questions:

Full data set
The full data set (n = 436) in this example include repeat observations throughout winter hibernation. For example, Tortoise #1 hibernated in the same burrow in 6 years of observation reflected in the first variogram (for 85-112 days/winter = ~12-16 consecutive observations).
The periodogram for the full data set confirms an annual peak (attached).
The ML estimate of the full home range, from 3 Jul 1996 to 26 Jul 2005, is only 2.8 ha. This tortoise did not move very far (similar to most in the study)!
Do the repeat observation during hibernation affect the output/interpretations? Any cautionary notes about interpreting AKDE estimates from this type of pattern?
Annual data set
The annual data sets exclude the hibernation period. They begin with the last date the tortoise was observed in its hibernaculum and end with the first date it was observed in its subsequent hibernaculum, so the only repeat (consecutive) observations within the year occur if/when the tortoise did not move between weekly observations during the activity period - which may have occurred during nesting or aestivation, but not as long as during hibernation (for most tortoises).
The ML estimate of the 2003 home range (n = 30 pts), from 31 Jan 2003 to 6 Nov 2003, is 3.2 ha.
The attached AKDE/points/path figure for this year shows that there was not a particular "shift" in the range during the year.
Are the model results/AKDE estimates reliable for this type of pattern?
Model selection
I do recall from your publications that the OU and OUF models are nested. I wonder what you think about applying the more common OU model across the board if that appears to be the "general case" vs applying the OUF (or IID) models for the particular cases where they might narrowly outcompete the OU model. That is, if the OU is the best-supported model most of the time out of dozens of individuals/years, does it make biological sense to apply an OUF or IID model in a handful of individual instances?
Periodograms
I still need to digest the full vignette, but I am having trouble interpreting the example. In the example, you state that "the periodogram exhibits a clear peak for the period of one day, but this is also present in the (red) diagnostic." To me, it looks like the example peaks at about the 2-week period, while the red diagnostic peaks at a month or so - unless you're looking for individual points instead of tracking the overall mass of points. Any tips for interpreting these graphs?

Thanks!
Roy

