Chris,
Following up on my questions about tortoise #1 and what to do with non-stationarity caused by hibernation periods in the longer-term datasets, attached are fitted variograms to the complete datasets compared to fitted variograms with the cumulative active-season datasets from each year (i.e., last hibernation point through first hibernation point of each each combined across all years). N=436 for the full dataset compared to n = 280 for the segmented and combined active-season dataset; the difference is almost entirely a result of subtracting all the intermediate hibernation records but also reflects dropping a few records at the beginning and end of the total dataset during which the tortoise was not tracked for a full season (i.e., the end of 1996 and beginning of 2005).

I also attached the corresponding periodograms. To me it looks like the periodicity in the active-season variogram is "roughed up" quite a bit, if not completely eliminated compared to the smooth pattern from the full dataset. Likewise, sparse data notwithstanding, the active-season periodogram does not seem to have the same peak at the 1-year mark as in the full dataset (maybe? ...). It looks like there might be a peak just before the 1-year mark, but it's matched by the sampling regime (which bounces up even higher on its own at the 1-year mark).

So, my current list of questions are as follows:
1) How does this combined active-season dataset look for drawing inferences?
2) Are there problems using the stationary models for inference in annual models that show a peak mid-way through the variogram rather than maintaining an asymptote? Do I need to include certain caveats in inferences drawn from that kind of pattern?
3) Thoughts about the discrepancy between the guestimated SVF for #58 (red) vs the fitted model (green)?

Thanks,
Roy

Roy

