OK, I think I might be getting closer to being able to run some valid models. Referring back to the numbered list of questions:
1) Hooray! I'll proceed with dropping the internal hibernation locations in the multi-year analyses. (I only dropped the incomplete seasons for the variograms in my last message as a matter of convenience in how I had already broken data out for the annual analyses. For this exercise, it was quicker and easier just to combine each full season because they already omitted repeat hibernation observations. I'll add all the active season records back in now that I have a path forward.)

2) I'm at a loss about what to do with annual data that produce patterns like "F#1 variogram_2003". The annual datasets already exclude the repeat hibernation records. I thought maybe inclusion of a nesting or summer dormancy burrow where the tortoise spends several consecutive weeks might be driving the pattern, but dropping those repeat observations did not fundamentally change the variogram. Out of ~80 annual datasets that I've looked at so far, ~16 show peak more or less instead of fluctuating around an asymptote. For #1 in 2003, this all occurs within an area of just 3.2 ha (AKDE based on the OU model - for both the 2003 data, as is, and the multi-year data; see "F#1 2003 AKDE_pts_path" from my Feb 16 post). Do I have any options (the goal is to describe variation in annual space use within individuals and overlap between individuals)?

3) Yep, the variogram starts creeping up after about 4 years, reaching a broad peak around 8 years. I guess my best option for this type of scenario is to use the variogram and GIS to describe the behavior, then break the dataset into behaviorally discrete chunks for more detailed exploration - unless you have multi-scale models to point me to.

It seems like I might be good with #1 and #3, but any additional guidance on #2 would be greatly appreciated. Thanks!
Roy

