> On Monday, February 19, 2018 at 5:14:43 PM UTC-8, Christen Fleming wrote:
> Hi Roy,
>
> I don't know that its necessary to drop the incomplete active seasons, unless there are some seasonal changes that happen to the home range that you need to average over.
>
> I'm not sure what I can see in the one periodogram. Maybe there is a peak at 1 year and its second harmonic (6 months) You might try averaging over multiple/all individuals.
>
> 1) The segmented variogram looks good.
> 2) Yes. The movement behavior is non-stationary with n=1 non-stationary events and the stationary model is time averaging over that, which is not very robust because n is small. One year might look good if you cut out hibernaculum, though.
> 3) I'd wager that if you zoom all the way out, the empirical variogram either creeps upwards or spikes upwards, which corresponds to a shift in the home range over time. You would need a multi-scale model to more accurately capture both the short-term and long-term ranging scales.
